You used structured bindings on map entries. They unpack any pair or tuple into named variables:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

std::pair<int, int> minMax(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    int lo = v[0];
    int hi = v[0];
    for (int x : v) {
        lo = std::min(lo, x);
        hi = std::max(hi, x);
    }
    return {lo, hi};
}

std::tuple<int, int, double> stats(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    const int count = static_cast<int>(v.size());
    const int sum = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);
    return {count, sum, static_cast<double>(sum) / count};
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> scores = {72, 95, 88, 64, 83};
    {{code}}
    std::cout << lo << ' ' << hi << '\n';
    std::cout << count << ' ' << sum << ' ' << mean << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
auto [lo, hi] = minMax(scores);        // a pair
auto [count, sum, mean] = stats(scores);  // a tuple
```

The number of names must match the number of slots. Under the hood, `auto [a, b] = p;` makes one hidden copy of `p`, and `a` and `b` name its slots. Changing `a` does not touch `p`. Write `auto& [a, b] = p;` to bind to the original, so changes reach it, or `const auto& [a, b]` to read without copying. A function result is a temporary, so `auto& [lo, hi] = minMax(scores);` does not compile: use `auto` or `const auto&` there.

This makes returning several values easy. Return a `std::pair` or `std::tuple` and build it with braces:

```cpp norun
std::pair<int, int> minMax(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    // ...
    return {lo, hi};
}
```

The returned object is built directly in the caller's variable, so nothing extra is copied. This is usually cleaner than reference out-parameters.

Bindings also work in range-for. `for (auto& [name, score] : entries)` names both halves of each pair by reference, so `score += 5` changes the vector. Use `const auto&` when you only read. Without the `&` each loop pass works on a copy and the vector stays the same.

